{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1554827946,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0MzEwNjU2OTQ=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3312","origin":"github"},"title":"status (and rev-save) treatment of the path is \"revolutionary\": we need to unify","message":"This is largely a follow up to #3230 (the overall mixup of semantics) and complement to #3282 (treatment of relative paths, not the case of absolute path and `None` dataset). I found a peace of mind through us agreeing somewhat (in #3230) on the semantic that specifying a path to a datalad command, without dataset explicitly, switches the context of the operation to that dataset.  That is how I believe many, primarily old, commands still operate (`install`, `get`, `uninstall`, `drop`, `add/save`, ...).  But `status` assumes current dataset as the \"context\" of operation, and then fails whenever target path is outside:\n\n```shell\n$\u003e datalad status /tmp/bmtemp/datalad_temp_bm_FQIGk4/testds10\n[ERROR  ] dataset containing given paths is not underneath the reference dataset \u003cDataset path=/home/yoh/proj/datalad/datalad\u003e: [] [status(/tmp/bmtemp/datalad_temp_bm_FQIGk4/testds10)] \n```\nsimilarly (may be just because of use of `status` inside) for `rev-save`\n```shell\n$\u003e datalad rev-save /tmp/bmtemp/datalad_temp_bm_FQIGk4/testds10 \n[ERROR  ] dataset containing given paths is not underneath the reference dataset \u003cDataset path=/home/yoh/proj/datalad/datalad\u003e: [] [status(/tmp/bmtemp/datalad_temp_bm_FQIGk4/testds10)] \n```\n\nIf we switch to such semantic it would become impossible to `install` a dataset or obtain (`get`) content without specifying on which dataset (i.e. providing `-d`) to operate.","files":null}]}